---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const kernelFamilies = [
  {
    name: 'B-splines',
    package: 'bsplines',
    description: 'Evaluate basis functions, derivatives and tensor-product shape functions on one-, two- and three-dimensional grids.',
  },
  {
    name: 'FEEC',
    package: 'feec',
    description: 'Project fields into compatible finite-element spaces, apply mass operators and assemble local contributions.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Geometry',
    package: 'geometry',
    description: 'Evaluate mappings between logical and physical coordinates, transform fields and compute geometry factors.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Linear algebra',
    package: 'linear_algebra',
    description: 'Apply distributed stencil matrices and their transposes to the coefficient vectors used by field solvers.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Particles',
    package: 'pic',
    description: 'Push markers, evaluate fields at marker positions, deposit moments and currents, filter data and sort markers.',
  },
];

const workflow = [
  { step: '01', title: 'Write', text: 'A kernel is a small, typed Python function using the NumPy subset supported by Pyccel.' },
  { step: '02', title: 'Discover', text: 'The compiler finds modules whose filenames contain “kernels” and records their dependencies.' },
  { step: '03', title: 'Compile', text: 'Pyccel translates the functions to Fortran or C and builds shared libraries for the selected compiler.' },
  { step: '04', title: 'Call', text: 'Struphy calls the compiled function through the same Python-facing module used by the simulation.' },
];

const compileCode = `struphy compile                  # compile kernels with Fortran
struphy compile --language c    # compile kernels with C
struphy compile --openmp        # enable OpenMP in the kernels
struphy compile --status        # show the compilation status
struphy compile --dependencies  # print kernels and dependencies`;
---

<Layout
  title="Struphy kernels — Struphy"
  description="The compiled Python kernels behind Struphy: B-spline evaluation, compatible finite elements, geometry, linear algebra and particle-in-cell operations."
>
  <main>
    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1 id="page-title">Struphy kernels</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Struphy keeps the simulation and model logic in readable Python, while moving the tight loops over spline
        coefficients, grid cells and particles into small computational kernels. These functions are written once in
        Python and can run as Python during development or as compiled Fortran or C in a production simulation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="role-title">
      <h2 id="role-title">What is a kernel?</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A kernel is a focused numerical routine that applies the same operation to many values: evaluate a basis
          function, multiply a stencil matrix by a vector, move a marker, or add marker weights to a grid. Kernels
          do not assemble a simulation or choose a time-stepping scheme. Propagators and models call them as the
          inner operations of a substep.
        </p>
        <p>
          This separation keeps the high-level API expressive without putting Python's per-element loop overhead in
          the hot path. It also gives each operation a small, testable interface and makes the data movement and
          parallel loops explicit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="families-title">
      <h2 id="families-title">Kernel families</h2>
      <div class="families">
        {kernelFamilies.map((family) => (
          <article class="family">
            <h3>{family.name}</h3>
            <code>struphy.{family.package}.*_kernels</code>
            <p>{family.description}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Particle-in-cell operations are split into pushing, sampling, accumulation, filtering and sorting modules.
        The <a href="/numerics/">numerical methods</a> page explains how these operations couple finite-element
        fields to marker distributions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
      <h2 id="workflow-title">From Python to a simulation</h2>
      <div class="workflow">
        {workflow.map((item) => (
          <article class="workflow-item">
            <span class="step">{item.step}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.text}</p>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Compilation is incremental: Struphy stores the discovered kernel list and rebuilds the generated code
          when a source or dependency changes. The language, compiler family and OpenMP option are selected at
          compile time. If a kernel has not been compiled, its Python implementation remains available, which is
          useful for debugging and for tests.
        </p>
      </div>
      <pre><code>{compileCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="performance-title">
      <h2 id="performance-title">Parallel execution</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Kernels are the layer where local work is parallelized. OpenMP can split loops over particles, cells or
          other independent entries on one node. MPI distributes the field grid and markers between ranks; the
          surrounding Struphy code handles marker migration, ghost-region exchange and reductions between kernel
          calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          Compiled kernels do not change the mathematical method. The same spline basis, discrete operators and
          particle-to-grid rules are used in the Python and compiled paths, so compilation is a performance choice
          rather than a separate numerical scheme.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          See <a href="/implementation/">Code and parallelization</a> for the package layers, MPI decomposition,
          clones, profiling and the current GPU roadmap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section next" aria-labelledby="next-title">
      <h2 id="next-title">Related pages</h2>
      <div class="next-grid">
        <a href="/implementation/"><h3>Code and parallelization</h3><p>How kernels fit into the package and distributed execution.</p></a>
        <a href="/numerics/"><h3>Numerical methods</h3><p>The discretizations implemented by the kernels.</p></a>
        <a href="/propagators/"><h3>Propagators</h3><p>The simulation substeps that call kernels.</p></a>
        <a href="/simulation/"><h3>Building a simulation</h3><p>Configure and run a model using the compiled library.</p></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro{max-width:820px;padding-block:clamp(56px,8vw,92px)}
  .eyebrow{margin:0 0 7px;color:var(--muted);font:600 .76rem/1.4 var(--mono);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}
  h1{margin:0 0 20px;font-size:clamp(2.35rem,5vw,4rem);line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-.035em}
  .standfirst{color:var(--muted);font-size:1.12rem;line-height:1.6}
  .section{padding-block:44px 62px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}
  h2{margin:0 0 20px;font-size:clamp(1.4rem,2.6vw,1.85rem)}
  h3{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem}
  .prose{max-width:740px}
  .prose p{margin:0 0 16px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.68}
  .prose p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .prose a,.note a{color:var(--cyan)}
  .families{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(250px,1fr));gap:1px;max-width:960px;background:var(--rule);border:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .family{padding:20px 22px;background:var(--ink)}
  .family h3{color:var(--cyan)}
  .family code{color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
  .family p{margin:12px 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.55}
  .note{max-width:740px;margin:20px 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:.88rem}
  .workflow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);max-width:960px;margin:28px 0 30px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .workflow-item{display:flex;gap:14px;padding:20px 18px 22px 0}
  .workflow-item+.workflow-item{padding-left:18px;border-left:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .step{color:var(--cyan);font:650 .78rem var(--mono)}
  .workflow-item p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.5}
  .aside{margin-top:20px!important;padding-left:14px;border-left:2px solid var(--cyan);font-size:.9rem}
  pre{max-width:820px;margin:24px 0 0;padding:20px 22px;overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule);background:var(--code-bg);color:var(--paper);font:.85rem/1.7 var(--mono)}
  .next-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:14px;max-width:900px}
  .next-grid a{display:block;padding:16px 18px;border:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--paper);text-decoration:none}
  .next-grid a:hover{border-color:var(--cyan);background:var(--accent-wash)}
  .next-grid h3{margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--cyan);font-size:.95rem}
  .next-grid p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:.82rem;line-height:1.5}
  @media(max-width:760px){.workflow{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.workflow-item:nth-child(3){border-left:0;padding-left:0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}.workflow-item:nth-child(4){border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}}
  @media(max-width:480px){.workflow{grid-template-columns:1fr}.workflow-item,.workflow-item+.workflow-item{padding:16px 0;border-left:0}.workflow-item+.workflow-item{border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}}
</style>
